---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy policy · upnexty"
  description="How upnexty uses your data: all data stays on the device. Nothing is sent to the developer."
>
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="lede">Effective 2 October 2026</p>

  <h2>Summary</h2>
  <p>
    upnexty keeps all of your data on the device. There is no upnexty server,
    no user account, and no analytics. The developer does not receive, store,
    or see any of your data.
  </p>

  <h2>Data from Google</h2>
  <p>
    If you connect a Google account, upnexty asks for one permission:
    <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.readonly</code>.
    This gives read-only access to your calendars.
  </p>
  <p>upnexty uses this access only to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>get the list of calendars in your account, so you can select which ones to show;</li>
    <li>get the events on those calendars, so the device can show them on its display.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    upnexty does not change, add, or delete events. It does not read your email,
    contacts, files, or any other Google data.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your data is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The device connects to Google directly. No other computer is between them.</li>
    <li>Your Google access token is stored in the device's internal flash memory.</li>
    <li>Calendar events are kept in the device's memory only to draw the display.</li>
    <li>No data from Google is sent to the developer or to any third party.</li>
    <li>No data is sold, used for advertising, or used to train AI or machine-learning models.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Other network connections</h2>
  <p>The device also connects to these services. It sends them no Google data:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>, for the weather. It receives the location you set on the device.</li>
    <li><a href="https://zenquotes.io/">ZenQuotes</a>, for a short quote at the end of the day. It receives no personal data.</li>
    <li>Any iCal feed URLs that you add yourself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to remove your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>On the device's settings page, push <strong>Disconnect</strong> for the account. This deletes the token from the device.</li>
    <li>You can also remove upnexty's access at <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>.</li>
    <li>If you reflash the device, all data on it is erased.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Google API Services User Data Policy</h2>
  <p>
    upnexty's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
    including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website has no cookies, no analytics, and no tracking. It is hosted on
    GitHub Pages, which can keep standard server logs.
  </p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>
    All of upnexty's code is public on <a href="https://github.com/nathanstitt/upnexty">GitHub</a> under the MIT license.
    You can examine it to make sure that it does what this policy says.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version is put on this page with a new effective date.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Nathan Stitt, <a href="mailto:nathan@argosity.com">nathan@argosity.com</a></p>
</Base>
